ntial race firmly for Republicans", "No public general-election polls or expert ratings; the forecast rests on fundamentals", "National reporting shows the president's approval remains positive in only about ten states, likely including Arkansas", "Democratic Southern outreach targets 2026 down-ballot races more than the presidential ticket"], "rationale": "Arkansas's deep Republican lean at the presidential level, with no polling, ratings, or spending signals suggesting movement, keeps the Republican nominee a clear favorite. The absence of direct measurement leaves uncertainty elevated but does not justify moving off the fundamentals.", "watch": "A sharp shift in the national political environment or the emergence of an unusually strong Democratic nominee could tighten the margin, though nothing in the current evidence points that way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Arkansas remains one of the most Republican-leaning states in presidential elections, and the partisan fundamentals point firmly to the Republican nominee. A lean of roughly fifteen points to the right of the country sets a high bar for any Democrat, and no public general-election polls or expert ratings this cycle suggest the gap has narrowed. A national report that the president's approval is positive in just ten states, likely including Arkansas, reinforces that advantage.\n\nDemocrats have begun investing in the South, with Kentucky's governor rallying Arkansas Democrats and arguing the party can compete and win in the region, but those efforts center on 2026 down-ballot contests rather than the presidential ticket. Commentary about Republicans betting on Trumpism without Trump hints at possible friction, yet nothing measurable indicates movement. With